<br />
 
<br />
The return to Rallos Zek 
<br />

<br />
Winter was arriving early. Though only early October the trees in Nektulos had already taken on that black, spooky lifeless look; and the cold ground was so covered with thousands of crackling brown leaves, that not even the well worn paths to neriak were visible. Young tier'dal fresh to the world outside of the city's gates were shouting that they were lost, and the undead that favored the night was already starting to rise from the ground. 
<br />

<br />
Kaldarye Ka'Duceus shivered and pulled the thick folds of her cloak around her. The biting wind promised a dank cold night. She was glad she was not spending another winter here on the dramaless legends. Soon they would be returning home. Her face looked slightly pleased, she was going back to the ways of treachery and revenge. She had a score to settle with a certain tier'dal blood prelate and game to end with her husband. When she returned things were going to change. 
<br />

<br />
The wind stirred the leafless branches above her with a howling whistle. Kaldarye kicked her boots in a pile of leaves and looked around. Her cerulean eyes easily pierced the dense brush between the inky trees. Carefully she listened to the screams and shouts of the lost newbs hoping to pick out a certain familiar cry, and not hearing the voice she hoped, she trampled away from Neriak towards eastern commons. Innoruuk's nails the child was wandering further and further away these days. 
<br />

<br />
Feeling highly annoyed she sent a quick tell, only to have it go unanswered. That she supposed was her own fault, for allowing the girl a day to wander and be just herself. With anyone else under her control Kaldarye never would have granted such a request, but Keeah had a way of holding her young tier'dal head and blinking those large blue innocent eyes, not to mention that Kaldarye had been ribaldly sick at the time of the request and only too glad not to have that little one scampering around. When she told Keeah she could go out and play for an entire day, she had no idea that the little one would leave neriak, and worse turn off her telepathic link. 
<br />

<br />
The lack of forest paths did little to hinder Kaldarye's passage. As a high priestess of Innoruuk, she was well familiar with the pitfalls of Nektulos and easily slipped around them. She was well aware though, that her child had no idea of the dangers outside the city and was likely deep in trouble. 
<br />

<br />
For a moment Kal's face looked pensive. She had to find that child before anything happened. That child was key to her scheme of revenge. Teir'dal children were of weak constitution and rezzing their spirits, should they meet an untimely end, was near impossible. A fact Kaldarye knew well enough but with the sick feeling she'd been having more and more often lately, it was easier to give in to her child's requests to leave the house and play, rather than force her to stay indoors and study. 
<br />

<br />
Getting too intelligent was something Kaldarye did not want her child to do. Kaldarye had plans for Keeah and those plans meant Keaah's tier'dal education was not a priority. Now it seemed though that the lack of priorities was causing this mess. She zoned into Eastern Commons and scanned the cloudy sunless sky. 
<br />

<br />
This trouble likely started 3 days ago, when Keeah first left. It had taken over a day for Kaldarye to realize Keeah was not coming home and another day to figure out she was not in any gate of the city. Torn between rage and guilt Kaldarye had spent the third day contacting members of Vilamil's guild, Redemption, trying to locate the rogue and also gathering up gear to travel. Now at the end of the third day she was tramping through the forest doing something she handn't done since she came to Legends – traveling outside the tier'dal homeland. 
<br />

<br />
She slowed her pace as she approched the boarders of Western Commons. Vilamil was meeting her at the Western Common's Druid ring where they'd catch a port to the nexus. From there they'd go to Plane of Knowledge where'd they'd start searching each zone one by one, looking for traces of her daughter. 
<br />

<br />
Kaldarye dreaded the lecture she was sure the rogue would give her. Vilamil highly disapproved of the way she was raising the child and she knew he would blame her for what had happened. And if anything had happened to the little tier'dal, Kaldarye was afraid the rogue would turn his back on her. 
<br />

<br />
And losing Vilamil's support was something she could not let happen. She needed Vilamil still to help her obtain her vegeance.
<br />
_________________
