<br />

<br />
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
<br />
 
<br />
“You are ruining that child.” Vilamil said as he dropped his adventuring pack in the middle of the stone kitchen floor. 
<br />

<br />
“Keeah? She's doing exactly as I wish.” Kaldarye replied. 
<br />

<br />
Vilamil glowered at her. “You don't even know what she's doing.” 
<br />

<br />
Kaldarye shrugged. “What does it matter. She's a happy child.” 
<br />

<br />
“She's too happy for a tier'dal. Too trusting of the lighties.” Vilamil growled. 
<br />

<br />
Kaldarye turned away and stirred the pot of wine, ladling out a tall mug for him. “She takes after you. She hears about you running around with the lighties in Redemption.” 
<br />

<br />
“She doesn't hear those stories from me. You're the one that has her thinking the lighties are my best friends.” 
<br />

<br />
Kaldarye met his eyes and felt a twinge of guilt. He was right. She had painted Keeah this beautiful picture of the lightie friends of “uncle Vil” and his life outside of Neriak. 
<br />

<br />
“You should hire that girl a proper tier'dal tutor if you're not going to teach her yourself.” 
<br />

<br />
“Our people are too rough. Keeah's a gentle child.” Kaldarye replied setting the mug of wine down in front of him hoping he'd drink it. 
<br />

<br />
Vil pushed the wine away and frowned. “She'll never survive Neriak in Rallos Zek.” 
<br />

<br />
“She does fine in Neriak here.” 
<br />

<br />
He stood suddenly and pushed her against the wall pining her there with his hand on he throat. Kaldarye was on her guard, knowing with one twist of his wrist he could snap her neck. His voice came out calm and deadly. “But Neriak on Rallos Zek is different and you know it. I doubt even your husband Greon would approve of the way you've raised this child without any form of hate or caution. Your daughter acts like a fricken happy halfling.” 
<br />

<br />
Kaldarye met his eyes. “I know what I'm doing.” 
<br />

<br />
“Do you?” he said tightening his grip on her throat making it difficult for her to breathe. “Sometimes I think that acid has muddled up more than your face.” 
<br />

<br />
Ignoring his insult Kaldarye gasped, “If your going to choke me, then Innoruuk's balls, do so without the lesson on parenting. What do you know about raising children anyway?” 
<br />

<br />
"Enough to know that tier'dal children aren't raised to love and laugh." 
<br />

<br />
He squeezed her throat shut giving her no breath to reply and added, "It's a wonder Innoruuk still answers your prayers and grants you your spells." 
<br />

<br />
Kaldarye desperetly tried to breathe but his grip was unrelenting. Just when she thought she'd pass out, he released his grip enough so that she could catch her breath. “No priestess, I won't choke you, though it's tempting to give you the whipping I gave your daughter.” 
<br />

<br />
“You beat her?” 
<br />

<br />
He chucked and released her throat, stepped back, and went over to the wine. “A public one in Plane of Knowledge. She screamed like a little girl. She won't want to sit down for days.” 
<br />

<br />
"She is a little girl." Kaldarye glared at him furiously. How could he do this so close to her goal. She couldn't have Keeah sullon and misrable when they returned to Rallos. It could put all her perfectly cultured plans in jeopardy. “How could you?” 
<br />

<br />
“Not so little of a girl anymore. She earned it, more times than I can count and I knew you wouldn't give her grief when I returned her.” He took a swallow of the wine and then continued, “and you know what she said to me when it was over and she was finished crying?” His eyes had a look of total . “She told me that she knew I was angry and that she would FORGIVE me. She said that in front of 3 other tier'dal in my guild.” He threw his entire goblet of wine into the kitchen hearth causing the low burning flames to pop and explode with orangish brightness of the fire pits of lavastorm. 
<br />

<br />
“At least she doesn't hate you.” Kaldarye replied with relief. 
<br />

<br />
“She doesn't hate anything. Your child has a good alignment. Kaldarye what have you done?” 
<br />

<br />
“Exactly what I planned the moment I knew she'd be born.” 
<br />

<br />
His voice was hard. “When we return to Rallos Zek she'll never pass the blood test of hate.” 
<br />

<br />
Kaldarye's voice grew stoney. “I know. I don't want her to pass.” 
<br />

<br />
Vilamil looked suprised. “Why not? Why would you of all tier'dal raise your own flesh and blood to fail.” 
<br />

<br />
Kaldarye's eyes flashed like deep blue topaz. “She's not my own flesh and blood. She's Tramplebrey's child. And I want to make sure that the child he forced out of me, the one he wants more than anything else, is a child he cannot possibly accept. A child that he will be forced to destroy.” 
<br />

<br />
Vilamil's voice was flat, his face was disgusted. “I think your a fool.” 
<br />

<br />
“I know what I'm doing.” Kaldarye replied. 
<br />

<br />
“Do you? How can you be so sure she's not Greon's?” 
<br />

<br />
“Because with Greon, there wasn't an opportunity.” 
<br />

<br />
“And the last child, the one you lost to the Hated Ones, there was?” 
<br />

<br />
Kaldarye inwardly groaned at the memory of the priest Senbula and the male child she'd lost to him. When they'd first arrived on legends they'd looked everywhere for that child but he was no where to be found. At least Vilamil had told her there was no trace of him. She glanced at the rogue, sometimes she'd wondered if he was lying. That was another life and time though. Now Kaldarye had other worries, other plans, the most important was getting vengeance on Tramplebrey, and the means to do so was right within her grasp. 
<br />

<br />
“Yes there was an opportunity. I didn't think it was successful though, til it was too late.” 
<br />

<br />
“And who's the father of the one your carrying now?” 
<br />

<br />
“No one. I'm not pregnant.” 
<br />

<br />
Vilamil looked as if he didn't believe her. “You are sick the way you were when we first came to Legends.” 
<br />

<br />
“That doesn't mean I'm with child. I can't be. Look at my face. It's still ruined by acid. What tier'dal male would have me?” 
<br />

<br />
“One who was paid perhaps.” 
<br />

<br />
Kaldarye slapped him. 
<br />

<br />
He rubbed his jaw and chuckled. “Priestess you are a terrible liar. With all females of any race, everyone knows that there is only one way for them to get with a child.” 
<br />

<br />
“Then I cannot possibly be with one.” she replied resisting the urge to slap him again knowing he wouldn't allow her to get away with it twice.
<br />
