<br />
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
<br />
 
<br />
The contents spilling out of the bag was a curious assortment. A few old dried iron rations, serveral small green stones with a metallic sheen, a few pieces of onyx, the remains of some dirty tattered gloves, and an old tier'dal rag doll that Keeah had been playing with since before she could crawl. There was also a small sheathed dagger, a high-quality fur-lined cloak that kaldarye had never seen her daughter wear, a ring looking suspiciously crafted of platinum, a fine pair of what kaldarye could only guess were dancing slippers, and a few goblets of wine and water. 
<br />

<br />
Kaldarye picked up the wine first. “Where did you get this?” 
<br />

<br />
Keeah didn't reply but looked as if she were ready to flee the room. Her huge blue eyes darted left and right and settled pleadingly on the rogue. 
<br />

<br />
“I imagine she got the wine from her adventuring companions in the Dreadlands.” Vilamil replied. 
<br />

<br />
Kaldarye glared at the rogue. “The Dreadlands? I don't believe it. She's too little to be anywhere near that zone. Look at this." she gestered to the pile, "She still plays with dolls. How could she possible survive the trip to that zone. Kaldarye smacked the goblet of wine back on the table too hard and cracking it, causing a dark leak of purple liquer to ooze out and stain the wooden table. 
<br />

<br />
Before the wine could ruin it, Vilamil lifted up the well loved toy that he had stolen in Freeport a long long time ago and picked at the crackling black paint he had covered it with to make it look tier'dal. The doll had originally reminded him of young Kaldarye before she was sent away from House N'Bane to live in the blood temple of Innoruuk. He'd kept it in his things, til the child discovered and claimed it. He had never intended to to become a well used tier'dal toy. 
<br />

<br />
“And she's too young to drink wine." Kaldarye continued in her naggy mother's voice. "At her age - I'll not allowed it.” 
<br />

<br />
Vilamil looked amused. He leaned back glanced at Keeah and grinned. “Whether or not you wished her to imbibe in alcoholic spirits, has made no difference.” 
<br />

<br />
“Of course it does, she's mine, she's bound to me. She wouldn't dare disobey.” 
<br />

<br />
Vil tossed his head back and laughed. “I warned you the loose un-tier'dal way she was raised would have consequences.” 
<br />

<br />
“Keeah wouldn't dare go against my wishes.” Kaldarye answered. 
<br />

<br />
“Why not? You've raised her without discipline. You've raised her to believe our race is joyous and happy. You've failed to instill her with distrust and fear. She thinks it's our duty to undo the injustices of the world, rather than be their major factor.” 
<br />

<br />
Kaldarye couldn't deny his words but she wasn't going to let him get in the last one either, “Someone else must have ensorceled her. She can't have run off to the Dreadlands like that on her own.” 
<br />

<br />
Vilamil pushed some of the greenish stones on the table towards her. “Someone has gotten to her, in a way you won't believe. While you've been ignoring her or letting her “play” your daughter as been testing her mettle against the beasties of Norrath.” 
<br />

<br />
“I haven't been ignoring her. I've been ill. Direly so for far too long. None of my healing remedies seems to fix it.” she replied. 
<br />

<br />
"Unknowing to you, while you've been preoccupied, your daughter has furthered her education quite nicely.” 
<br />

<br />
“Impossible, I made sure her education was never advanced. For my plans with Tramplebrey, I need her to stay simple. I've never even taken her beyond the city gates.” Kaldarye snapped, forgetting her daughter was in the same room. 
<br />

<br />
Vilamil grunted. "Well you're right on one thing. While you were ill, Someone else ensorcelled Keeah to some extent and took over her education. They trained her in an art I don't think House Ka'Duceus, or Tramplebrey or the blood temple ever intended. In fact I think you may have been made sick on purpose so that Innoruuk or someone else could interfere with his own brand of havoc.” 
<br />

<br />
“That is not possible. It's not Innoruuk. Innoruuk supports my plans for this child. If he didn't he wouldn't answer my prayers.” 
<br />

<br />
“Whether or not he supports you has nothing to do with what's already been done.” 
<br />

<br />
“What are you talking about? What could possibly have been done. All I've ever let her do, while I was taking naps, was go outside and play.” 
<br />

<br />
Vilamil picked up one of the metallic green stones. “Do you know what this is?” 
<br />

<br />
Kaldarye looked at the stone closely. “It looks like malachite. Practically worthless, doesn't sell for much.” 
<br />

<br />
“It's not worthless to a mage. It's the component used to summon a pet.” 
<br />

<br />
It took Kaldarye a moment to realize what he was suggesting, then like a slow rolling wave of hot tar, she realized what he meant. She turned towards her daughter, searching the child's face for any sign of denial or confusion. Finding none she glanced back at Vilamil in horror. 
<br />

<br />
“Your obedient joyful daughter has been trained as a mage.” Vilamil added.
<br />
