				<td colspan="2"><span class="postbody">Posted: Wed Nov 19, 2003 9:19 pm    Post subject: The Return to Rallos Zek    
<br />

<br />
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
<br />
 
<br />
“A mage.” Kaldarye shrieked. “It can't be. Both her parents are clerics. High priests, in Innoruuk's favor. Where would she get the ability as a mage.” 
<br />

<br />
“Your parents were both spellcasters and you were born a cleric.” Vilamil replied as if that answered the question. 
<br />

<br />
Feeling at a loss for words, Kaldarye spun towards her daughter. “Is it true?” she asked. 
<br />

<br />
Keeah shrunk away and cowered. “Yes, mama.” the little dark elf replied with her head down. 
<br />

<br />
“How, why? Who taught you.” Kaldarye demanded grabbing her daughters trembling chin and forcing the child to look at her. 
<br />

<br />
Kaldarye's mind was rolling. It took all her willpower not to slap her child. Keeah couldn't be a mage. This could throw all her plans with Tramplebrey away. Tramplebrey might welcome an heir trained in the arcane arts. It would tie Keeah closely to Kaldarye's father. Kaldarye had to stop this mage nonsense fast. She had to find a way to undo what was done. 
<br />

<br />
“Friends taught me, Mama. I had to do it to save them. I'm their only hope. I wanted to tell you but they warned me not to. They said you wouldn't understand.” 
<br />

<br />
“Friends? What friends?” Kaldarye shook her daughter trying to spill the words out. 
<br />

<br />
Keeah started crying. “Lizza and Toostit, they are high elven mages being held prisoner by Malsqeek the enchanter. They have to do whatever he says, they can't ever go home. They told me that if I help them and go out into the world to collect things for them that they can summon a key that will allow them to go free." 
<br />

<br />
“Slaves.” Vilamil chuckled. “You're daughter's being trained by another elf's slaves. Though doubtfully it's just the slaves. No doubt, for reasons of his own, their Tier'dal master is involved." 
<br />

<br />
"Why would you think that?" 
<br />

<br />
"Because Mages can't summon keys.” 
<br />

<br />
“Why would a tier'dal enchanter want to train my daughter?” Kaldarye said letting her daughter go and watching her scamper to the far side of the kitchen near the hearth. 
<br />

<br />
“Because she's innocent to our ways. She acts like a wood elf and could be grown into the perfect slave. A tier'dal slave, trained to please and obey with arcane powers of her own. Were she not your daughter, it would be tempting to take her for myself.” 
<br />

<br />
“You are mad.” 
<br />

<br />
Vilamil glared at her darkly. “Am I? You should now well enough that Earenhylle the necro keeps tier'dal slaves of his own. You don't seriously think he's the only one.” 
<br />

<br />
Kaldarye slammed her fist on the table outraged. “I would never allow it. I'm way more powerful than Malsqueek. If he tried to touch Keeah, he'd find himself smited.” 
<br />

<br />
“No he wouldn't. Not here on Legends. The rules here on killing are different. On Legends all he'd have to do is lure your daughter to his lair and once she's there and snapped into a slave collar she'd never change her mind and return home.” 
<br />

<br />
“Then why hasn't he done so already?” 
<br />

<br />
“She's too little and young. If he took her now, as her mother, if you demanded her return, he'd be forced to give it In a few more years though, that will change. And Malsqueek will be able to challenge your authority over her much faster now that he's provided the expense of Keeah's mage training.” 
<br />

<br />
“He won't have her, I won't let him. We'll be leaving here in two days.” 
<br />

<br />
“No. He won't, but on Rallos Zek you'll have an even greater problem with the way your daughter acts. You're authority can be challenged directly. You do realize that most of the tier'dal who end up as another elf's slave were raised outside of tier'dal dictates. And once your not there to protect her, she'll find herself defenseless.” 
<br />

<br />
“Her life won't come to that. Within a month of returning she'll be going to the blood temple.” 
<br />

<br />
Vilamil's face grew stoney. “And if she passes?” 
<br />

<br />
Kaldarye glared at him but didnt' respond outloud, instead she replied in a private tell, “I have a month to undo what was done by that enchanter, and in that time I will make sure Tramplebrey can't possibly accept her.” 
<br />

<br />
Vilamil nodded glanced at Keeah then back at Kaldarye. His face was deadly. “If I gain proof that she's Tramplebrey's child, I'll make sure when she goes for her test of blood that she doesn't survive.” 
<br />

<br />
He glanced away from Kaldarye back to the child. "But if I find out shes not of the blood of your temple, her life, once she's grown to majority, will be bound for as long as I wish, to me." 
<br />

<br />

