<br />

<br />
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
<br />
 
<br />
The door to Malsqueeks house was opened by a tiny creature with a hunched-over back and gray pallid skin. A dirty hairnet draped over a mat of oily yellow hair. It wore a green dress-like thing and in one hand it carried a squeaky glowing lantern. The creature eye'd Kaldarye suspiciously. 
<br />

<br />
“Who be you and what be you wanting at this late hour.” The creature asked in a scratchy female voice. 
<br />

<br />
It took Kaldarye a moment to realize the mistreated creature was halfing. It was even uglier than her own face. She glared at it with open repulsion. What had the enchanter had done to twist this creature's spine so? It reminded her of something Tramplebrey would do; which only swelled her anger. It was Tramplebrey's fault she was here on Legends. Tramplebrey had made her pregnant again. And it was also his fault her face looked like something out of a troll's lair. Pushing past the decrepit slave, Kaldarye stood in the drafty stone entryway, and replied, “I'm seeking Malsqueek the enchanter.” 
<br />

<br />
“Hold on you can't go in there.” the little slave intoned haughtily as it tried to block Kaldarye's entrance. “My master is powerful with more powerful friends. What house in Neriak do you represent?” 
<br />

<br />
Kaldarye turned on the smaller creature and smacked it over the head with her shield causing it to fall to the floor in a crumpled heap. “I represent myself.” she replied as she pinched out the lantern's low glowing light. 
<br />

<br />
Not checking to see if the halfing was dead, Kaldarye moved deeper into the gloomy hallway. The ceiling was high but coated with a giant nest of webs that slung down from the walls like spiraling ethereal tendrils. Fat spiders zipped through the misty strands above her, occasionlly dropping the husks of a dead insect or mouse to the floor. Kaldarye felt like she was a newbie walking alone through Befallen. For a second she wished she had the halfing to guide her. The cooridor was one long hallway flanked by large wooden doors on either side at uneaven intervals. It was drafty, cold and silent. 
<br />

<br />
Not wanting to spend anymore time here than she had to, Kaldarye opened the first wooden door into a drabby parlor. Despite the exterior of the home's gothic formality this room was barren and shabby. It was also empty except for a large fat rat with glowing yellow eyes that scuttled around a crumbling fireplace. Kaldarye closed the door quickly. Some spell casters were known to keep familiars and a rat seemed the perfect companion for the enchanter. 
<br />

<br />
She felt a deep satisfaction as she entered the hallway again. the enchanter wasn't as wealthy as he led the rest of Neriak to perceive. And Kaldarye knew with the right spells she could slay him. 
<br />

<br />
At the next door she pressed her ear against the gnarled wood. Hearing nothing she moved past it without opening the door. But at the third door on the opposite side of the cooridor, Kaldarye heard voices. 
<br />

<br />
She listened for a moment and her eyes blazed. One of the voices was Keeah. What was her daughter doing at Malsqueek's house at this late hour? 
<br />

<br />

