				<td colspan="2"><span class="postbody">Posted: Sat Nov 29, 2003 7:05 pm    Post subject: The Return to Rallos Zek    
<br />

<br />
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
<br />
 
<br />
Kaldarye pressed her ear as hard as she could to the dark wooden door and listened. 
<br />

<br />
“My mama and uncle Vil say we have to leave town, but I really don't want to.” Keeah said unhappily. 
<br />

<br />
“Where are you going to go?” an older velvety female voice asked. 
<br />

<br />
“They say we're going home, Lissa, to someplace called Rallos Zek.” 
<br />

<br />
There was a long pause. Then the female voice named Lissa said, “It is good that you came and told me. Rallos Zek is very far from here. So far that had you gone I would never have seen you again.” 
<br />

<br />
“I don't know where it is, but if I don't go quietly Uncle Vil is sure to whip me again. He's already threatened to take my doll away if I make any sound tomorrow.” Keeah sounded frantic. 
<br />

<br />
“Whip you and take your doll? That's terrible. You're so little and so good. He must be an evil dark elf.” 
<br />

<br />
Keeah let out a small sob. "It hurt bad. I've never been whipped before. My mama never hits me.” 
<br />

<br />
“Your mama sounds like my good high elven mama. Don't worry child, I'll protect you.” 
<br />

<br />
“You can't protect me. And I so wanted to help you and Toosit. Now I won't be able to and you'll both always be Malsqueek's slaves.” 
<br />

<br />
“No no, you must stay here. We are soo close to getting what we want. There must be a way to keep you with us.” 
<br />

<br />
"There's no way. We always do what uncle Vilamil says because he pays for the house we live in." 
<br />

<br />
Lissa sounded thoughtful. “He is your uncle and not your master then? so your not bound to him?" 
<br />

<br />
"No. I've always called him Uncle Vil. Mama calls him Vil or Vilamil." 
<br />

<br />
"Pay attention this is important." Lissa almost snapped. "Is your mother deferential to him?" 
<br />

<br />
"No. Most of the time she's angry at him. They fight a lot." 
<br />

<br />
"This is good, little one, very good. I think I know of a way to help you." 
<br />

<br />
"You do?" 
<br />

<br />
What if I gave you a magic item that would allow you to stay with us so you could help us complete our mission of freedom?” 
<br />

<br />
Keeah sounded hopeful. “I'd like that. I really want to help you.” 
<br />

<br />
“And if I can arrange it tonight little one, then you won't have to leave.” 
<br />

<br />
Keeah's voice turned crestfallen. “But what about my mama? She won't be happy if I don't go with her, and I don't want to leave her for a long time. I'll miss her.” 
<br />

<br />
Lissa's voice turned cunning. “Your mama is just a simple dark elf with no skills. Maybe I can get your mama one of these items too so she won't have to leave either. We can hide you in the cellar here where no one, especially not your uncle Vil will ever find you.” 
<br />

<br />
“You can do that? Hide us from Malsqueek?” 
<br />

<br />
“Of course. Little One. Malsqueek never ventures into the cellar. It's too dark for him. He prefers places that are light and airy.” 
<br />

<br />
Kaldarye stiffened. She knew Lissa was lying about the enchanter not liking the dark. His house was proof enough he adored the shadows. But why would Lissa the slave lie? There was really only one way to find out. Taking her invisiblity mask out of her bag, Kaldarye activated one of it's precious charges and disappeared. Then she cracked open the door and took a peak inside. 
<br />

<br />
Lissa sat on a dilapidated chaise with her back to Kaldarye. Her fine silvery hair was pulled up in a top knot and snaking down her back. Keeah sat on the chaise as well, near the high elf, looking innocent and hopeful. 
<br />

<br />
Kaldarye slipped into the room and moved invisibly through the shadows. 
<br />

<br />
“What do I have to do so mama and I can stay here?” Keeah asked. 
<br />

<br />
“You must do as I say. I'll get you a collar to wear little one. All you have to do is put it on and then put an identical one on your mother while she's sleeping. Then wake your mother up and lead her here. It will be very very easy. I'll make up a nice comfortable place in the cellar for you both to stay in.” 
<br />

<br />
“A collar, like the slave collar you wear?” Keeah asked alarmed. 
<br />

<br />
“No no not exactly like it. You're a dark elf so you wont' be affected by it like I am. Dark elves can't control other dark elves. It's not allowed here on Legends. The collar for you will just provide a little magical charge so your Uncle Vilamil can't track you.” 
<br />

<br />
“Oh good.” Keeah sounded relieved. “And then I can stay and help you and Toosit get your freedom. "You think this will really work?” 
<br />

<br />
“Oh little one. I know it will. Now stay here, and I'll get you the collars.” Lissa said as she stood up. She turned and faced the invisible Kaldarye. 
<br />

<br />
For a moment all Kaldarye saw was a tall graceful high elven female with shimmering silvery hair but then Kaldarye's true sight kicked in and Kaldarye saw the image of a tier'dal female beneath the facade of high elvenhood. 
<br />

<br />
Kaldarye felt her blood race. Lissa was wearing an illusion. 
<br />

<br />

