<br />

<br />
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
<br />
 
<br />
An hour after sending the message to Anuxiree, Vilamil was walking down a shadowy cobbled street in Neriak towards Malsqueek the Enchanter's house. He moved quickly, staying within the lightless shadows on the opposite side of the pavement, observing Malsqueeks old gothic house as he approached. It stood amidst a brushy thicket like a tomb. There was not even the faintest light beyond any of the small bullet shaped front windows. 
<br />

<br />
Moving two blocks past the house, he turned, walked around another block and observed Malsqueek's house from the rear. He was looking for guards and another possible entry. He didn't think Kaldarye would get herself into any serious trouble if indeed she had come to the enchanter's house but he wasn't taking any chances. And as he crept towards the house from the rear, he was glad for the caution. The house was indeed guarded by an Air Elemental pet and several Neriak Guards. 
<br />

<br />
Vilamil crouched down next to a large phosphlorescent violet Lichen plant on the edge of the property and observed the guards. There were more guards posted than he expected, setting his tier'dal insticts on edge, but most were standing in relaxed stance, swords sheathed, occasionally pacing as they watched the property. The elemental was stationary in the yard's single foggy light. 
<br />

<br />
As he observed them, a door opened in the wall and a sargent poked his head out and gave order for a few of the guards to follow him inside. Vilamil waited to see if anything happened after the guards entered but the house remained dark and lightless. He moved away from the glowing lichen into the deeper shadows of the thicket, and looked behind him making sure he wasn't being followed and no alarm was set. 
<br />

<br />
Vilamil continued sneaking towards the rear of the house and climbed over a small locked gate. Again he crouched down next to a bush and looked up surveying the house. From this vantage he could see a small candle burning behind a window on the third floor. He waited as a shadow of an elf appeared briefly over the flame. Someone was up there. Someone dangerous was waiting. His rogue instincts could sense it. 
<br />

<br />
He crept around the side of the house away from the guards. The left side of the house had only a single window on the third floor, and a serious of bricked up windows lower to the ground. Doubling back behind the guards, he surveyed the right side. To his disappointment the windows here were too small for him to slip though, they looked more like a widened arrow slits than a real windows. He was forced to move back to the front of the house. 
<br />

<br />
Innoruuk's balls he was not happy. 
<br />

<br />
He should have tied up Kaldarye and her daughter before they went to sleep. 
<br />

<br />
He should have known from the way Keeah had been whining something like this would happen. He should have known from the way Kaldarye had been behaving that she'd do something stupid. He couldn't believe that he'd let both of them slip through his fingers on the final night of Legends. 
<br />

<br />
Hatred boiled within him. The hatred was directed at his own self and at Kaldarye - who's future he had a vested interest in. It would be so easy to let Kaldarye and her impish child die, leave them behind. He wished he didn't care about them but he couldn't help it. 
<br />

<br />
Slinking around the corner of the house, Vilamil began to check the front of it for traces of magical alarms and traps. He had just found his first sign of a burnt out magical trap when a message came to him from Rallos from Greon..... 
<br />

<br />

