				<td colspan="2"><span class="postbody">Posted: Fri Dec 05, 2003 12:44 am    Post subject: The Return to Rallos Zek    
<br />

<br />
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
<br />
 
<br />
Kaldarye froze as Lissa looked right at her. She poised herself for a spell but the illusioned tier'dal's gaze went through her. Lissa moved past Kaldarye giving no indication that Kaldarye had been seen. She stopped at the door and turned back towards Keeah. “I'll be back in a few moments, little one.” 
<br />

<br />
Keeah nodded and got up from the chaise. And Kaldarye got the first good look at her daughter. Keeah was wearing the cloak and gear that had spilled out of the bag Vilamil had found on her. The cloak shifted her image in and out of the shadows which explained how she'd slipped out of the house unnoticed. The dirty old slippers took on an aura of graceful elegance. The wands that Kaldarye was sure Vilamil had confiscated glowed softly on her daughter's waist. If Kaldarye hadn't of been Keeah's mother she might have been admiring of the child's audacity but instead the sight of her child alone in this house set Kaldarye's anger on fire. 
<br />

<br />
Kaldarye barely noticed when Lissa stepped out of the room and closed the door behind her. 
<br />

<br />
Feeling the rage of Innoruuk ignite her bones she stepped out of the illusion of invisiblity, crossed the room, and for the first time in her life, smacked her daughter to the floor. 
<br />

<br />
Keeah screamed and reached for a wand as Kaldarye grabbed her daughter's tunic and plucked her off the ground. 
<br />

<br />
“Mama,” Keeah said in shocked recognition as she rubbed her cheek. 
<br />

<br />
Kaldarye pushed her daughter onto the chaise. Her eyes were glowing with fury. For once she was too angry to speak. It took all of her willpower not to pull out Keeah's hair by the roots. 
<br />

<br />
Looking slightly relieved and not realizing the imminent danger she faced from her own mother Keeah added, “What are you doing here, mama?” 
<br />

<br />
“I should ask you the same thing.” Kaldarye snapped as she curled her fingers back suppressing a spell. 
<br />

<br />
“I came to help my friends, Mama,” Keeah replied. 
<br />

<br />
"Friends?" Kaldarye's fingers were crackling with Innoruuk's hate. She balled her fists to keep from casting as the door to the room burst open. 
<br />

<br />
Forgetting Keeah, Kaldarye turned towards the opening as 5 neriak guards, Lissa and Malsqueek the Enchanter burst in. 
<br />

<br />
Kaldarye instantly cast her AE stun. To her surprise the magic bounced off the intruders harmlessly. Her daughter, however, instantly collapsed in shock from the blast. Kaldarye glanced at Keeah dumbfounded then back at the enchanter as she perpared to cast the same spell again. 
<br />

<br />
“I suggest you refrain from casting area of effect spells lest you wish to kill your own child.” Malsqueek replied with a smirk as he walked into the room. 
<br />

<br />
“You spineless hatefiend. How dare you train my daughter to be a mage.” Kaldarye spat as she called on Innoruuk's judgement to rain on his ass. Magical power crackled down from the ceiling and illuminated the enchanter in a blinding flash. Kaldarye smiled sardonically as the burst of light cleared and then frowned. The spell should have incinerated him, but Malsqueek stood before her looking bored and amused, as if nothing had happened. 
<br />

<br />
“But Keeah makes an excellent mage. She's full of unusual natural talent.” He said stepping away from the scorched spot on the floor. “Tell me is your family a line of spellcasters?” 
<br />

<br />
Ignoring his question, Kaldarye tried to blast him again. But he walked through her magical force harmlessly. 
<br />

<br />
Acting as if she weren't trying to kill him he said, “we had such a debate on what class of magic to train her in. We decided on mage though because here we have ample resources for the conjuring arcane." 
<br />

<br />
"You had no right." Kaldarye replied as she readied another spell. 
<br />

<br />
Malsqueek studied her gestures and added, "Or perhaps her innate sense of magic comes from the family of her father?” 
<br />

<br />
At the reminder of Tramplebrey, Kaldarye's eyes blazed. “Her father is a cleric.” Kaldarye said as she cast a root on him. 
<br />

<br />
He walked through the root with no effect. “Ahh an ancesteral throwback then for magic. It is lucky for the child she at least takes after her father in appearance. Were she to take after you, she'd be no better to me than a goblin slave." 
<br />

<br />
Kaldarye lost her control. Again she called on Innoruuk's Judgment then then his smite, then his anger til her mana was nearly exhausted and the floor around the enchanter's feet was blackened. 
<br />

<br />
Malsqueek looked at her and laughed. 
<br />

<br />
"An excellent display my dear....priestess is it?" 
<br />

<br />
Kaldarye glared at him and tried to will herself to gather more mana. 
<br />

<br />
Your powers of Innoruuk have no doubt has served you well on Rallos Zek. But here you will find no satisfaction in Neriak from it. Here on Legends force and might are served by power of a different kind." He steped back and nodded to the guards who encircled her. 
<br />

<br />
Kaldarye wasn't finished fighting. Whipping out her epic she prepared to beat them back. And for a moment she was able to get in a few blows but there were too many of them. They overwhelmed her. Before she knew it her hands were being manacled behind her back. She continued fighting til the guards beat her into unconsciousness. 
<br />

<br />
Once Kaldarye was unconscious the sargent glanced at the enchanter and said. “Her weapon won't leave her hand, shall I break it, milord?” 
<br />

<br />
Malsqueek looked down at Kaldarye humorlessly. “If you wish but, it wont' make any difference. She's from Rallos, no doubt all her gear is bound to her person. They have a way of doing that from her dimension. Her gear is harmeless to us though, she is a priestess. The most she can do is heal wounds.” 
<br />

<br />
For the first time since entering the room, Lissa stepped towards the enchanter and wispered into his ear. “Get the priestess out of here before her daughter recovers from her stun. I think I can still convince the child to adorn the collar and come into our control.” 
<br />

<br />
Malsqueek grinned evilly at Lissa then looked at the guard. “Sargent, remove the priestess from my presence.” 
<br />

<br />
“To where milord?” the guard asked. 
<br />

<br />
“To the city dungeon if you must. Her weapon is proof enough she's too powerful for me to control and her face is too abominable looking for me to want.” 
<br />

<br />
“As you wish, Milord, the sargent said as he drug the unconscious cleric towards the door.” 
<br />

<br />

<br />
