<br />

<br />
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
<br />
 
<br />
Greon paced in his house waiting the appointed hour when he would make contact with the shadowy minion of Kaldarye’s. He knew that to succeed he would have to be in a calm state of mind. He finally settled in his office and poured himself a brandy. Setting in his favorite chair he thought of every thing that had brought him to this point. 
<br />

<br />
It had been 6 months after that humiliating and totally frustrating day of Kaldarye’s mental cry for help when Greon had resignedly accepted the fact that he would no doubt never see either her or his child. But the day, of what he was sure was a kidnapping, was burned into his mind. And his desire for vengeance was still as strong. This may be for nothing, but at least is was something since that day. 
<br />

<br />
He had been lectured by the Prelate Perrir Zexus in the Temple and refused support when he had let it be know that he was going to storm into Kaldarye’s former House and the Blood temple and demand her release. He was sure one or the other had been instrumental in her disappearance. 
<br />

<br />
“You will not set foot into that compound if you value your life! I will see to your demise if you continue this foolish train of thought. Think of the repercussions on your House,” the prelate sneered, “and those on your guild and on my House. Our balance of power is not so sure that Tramplebrey or Kal’s father wouldn’t jump at the chance to humiliate the Temple and me.” 
<br />

<br />
Zexus paced like an angry cat in front of the fuming cleric, “You have, so far, removed yourself from the Great game, and now like a blind child you would rush in and stir a fire hornets nest! Think, Greon, think! You must have proof before you make accusations, or you risk the very thing you hope to save. I seriously doubt that either have taken Kaldarye as the Council has given sanction to the marriage after the fact. It would be political suicide. They took a violent blow to their plots as is, without courting more.” 
<br />

<br />
Greon couldn’t even articulate his frustration other than a growl. He stood before his Clerical superior whom he had bound his nascent House to less than a year ago, legs far apart in a battle stance and his arms clasped together. His hands were tightly clutching his arms as if he needed that grip to remind himself to not jump on the prelate and tear his throat out for refusing to support him. 
<br />

<br />
“All I ask for is a honor guard and a letter of introduction,” he gritted out finally. 
<br />

<br />
Zexus looked sardonically at Greon, “A guard of enough force to take the woman if she is there you mean, and a letter that would indicate that I was giving sanction to this action. I think not.” He returned to his desk and sat down steepling his hands in front of him, “I commend your holy rage at this action against your wife, but you are not powerful enough to even consider opening such hostilities. They would sweep you aside like a fire beatle. They would have then, for you two bollixing up their plans, if it wasn’t revealed the unmistakable Claws of Innoruuk in the whole affair.” 
<br />

<br />
What the elder cleric alluded to was the marriage and three day gestation and birth of the first child (if you could call it that) from Greon’s and Kaldarye’s coupling. Innoruuk had demanded a sacrifice from Kaldarye of a child of her and the man he designated- Greon. 
<br />

<br />
Unfortunately for the rest of the Teir`dals that were working there own plots with Kaldarye as a pawn, (who was oblivious to the part that she was suppose to play,) she with religious zeal entrapped Greon into the marriage and subsequent steel handed seduction of the emotionally scarred cleric with the help of Earenhylle (who had his own agenda). 
<br />

<br />
The plots and counter-plots of two Great Houses, Tramplebrey of the Blood Temple, the Council of Neriak and Kaldarye herself were brought to naught by the hand of Innoruuk. 
<br />

<br />
Kaldarye never got what she expected as reward for her service to Innoruuk, but was put through a three day torture that embittered her towards her god, stripping away her haughty pride of being special and chosen of her god. 
<br />

<br />
Greon’s arrogance and secretly harbored dreams were broken on Innoruuk’s altar as the renegade cleric was forced to acknowledge and submit to the Hate Lord, forced to dedicate his firstborn as a sacrifice, by his own hand, to save the life of Kaldarye and the guild mates that were in his care in Neriak (one of them being the gravid Viertania). 
<br />

<br />
The only good that might be seen from the mortal’s viewpoint was Earenhylle got more threads to weave in his plots. Kaldarye was saved from a political and detested marriage to Tramplebrey, and having been married, after the contract was ended, could no longer be forced by her father to marry whom he close. She was a free woman. And for the last pawn in Innoruuk’s game? Greon was no longer a virgin and found it good; his House was growing even though he had sacrificed on Innoruuk’s altar the peaceful idealist vision he had secretly harbored in his soul. 
<br />

<br />
But Innoruuk was not finished meddling in the lives of those in Neriak. The power brokers had to consider this new piece in the game and what it all meant. The two young clerics were not aware that many eyes watched them from the darkness of their subterranean towers, but they would be included into future calculations. It was yet to be seen if they were to be shunned, courted, or destroyed. 
<br />

<br />
None of this was known by the cleric though as, within his partially build house, he closed his eyes and slipped into a meditative trance. Like a fisherman casting, his telepathic line reeled out across the ether building a tenuous bridge to he whom he called… 
<br />

<br />
“Vilamel…”
<br />
