<br />
 
<br />
Vilamil followed the grotesque procession of Kaldarye draped atop an elemental and flanked by several Neriak guards. They moved quickly through the winding streets and back alleys of Neriak towards the nefarious wharf that housed the stone monolith known as the Halls of Injustice. The rogue tensed as the procession entered the prison. He'd been tempted to attack since they'd left Malsqueeks estate but had held himself in check, not wanting to draw attention. Even a rogue as skilled as he would have difficulty against 20 capable warriors. Following the guards inside the prison, however, was well within his abilities and he trailed behind and listened as they handed Kaldarye over to the prison ward. 
<br />

<br />
The warden was a gangly darkelf with a prominent birdline nose. His armor was ill fitting and worn. His gray eyes were droopy and bored and he gave Kaldarye a cursory glance and lifted up her bloody head by the hair so he could examine her face. He scowled. “What an ugly halfbreed. Where did your master find this thing?” 
<br />

<br />
“Tresspassing inside the estate.” the captain of Malsqueek's guard replied with scorn. 
<br />

<br />
The warden nodded as if he didn't care whether the story was true or not. “We're crowded. A new shipment of slaves arrived from east commons that still need to be branded and processed. What's this thing's importance.” 
<br />

<br />
“Highly. His lordship ordered us to take it away, and make sure it didn't return. It put up a decent fight.” 
<br />

<br />
Looking uninterested the warden released Kaldarye's head and replied, “Remove it to the temple for sacrifice, if he doesn't want it to bother him again, why bring it here.” 
<br />

<br />
The captain frowned. “I can't take it to the temple. This is a priestess of Innoruuk. An epic weapon is fused in her hand. The temple won't sacrifice her and definitely won't keep her against her will.” 
<br />

<br />
The warden replied eyes blazed. “Very well. But your master plays with fire.” he gestered to a pair of guards. “Take this thing down to a cell in the lower dungeons and deny it food for a few days. If no one enters its cell, there's no way it can bother Malsqueek.” 
<br />

<br />
Vilamil listened to the prison warden's orders. He chuckled. Whether the prison guards opened her door to feed her or not, getting Kaldarye out of this place would be easy. But first he intended to rescue little Keeah.
<br />
