<br />
 
<br />
Vilamil moved through the dark quiet streets of Neriak towards Malsqueeks estate. The whole nights affair weighed heavily on his mind. What was Malsqueek interest in an unknown unimportant tier'dal child. Keeah's parentage was a secret, he couldn't know about her direct involvement in the power play back on Rallos Zek. It must be something else. Something about the child, herself. Perhaps a spellcaster had some ability to detect something in her. He replayed his recent interactions with Keeah over in his mind as he left one dark ally and entered another. There didn't seem to be anything overtly interesting about the child other than being far too disobedient. She was a minor irritant that occcupied Kaldarye and kept her out of trouble. And would have continued as such had Kaldarye raised the child tier'dal. 
<br />

<br />
Vilamil frowned. The way Kaldarye handled her child was another puzzlement for the rogue. If Vilamil had his way, Keeah would be out of the way – locked somewhere with a dozen ancient tombs on Neriak history or some equally boring subject and not allowed out til she'd read them. Wasn't that the way most children were treated, trained to hate their life, their families, their existence - beaten into submission yet forced to endure. Childhood was something the strong were supposed to survive not enjoy. 
<br />

<br />
Vilamil cut across tavern street and into the older neighborhood where the chanter lived. Not for the first time, he wondered about the acid Tramplebrey had used on Kaldarye's face and if it had affected her mind. Hate bred strength. Hate brought honor. That code needed to be beaten into Keeah if she was to grow up a proper teir'dal. He paused in front the street on which Malsqueek's lived. But Kaldarye never intended for Keeah to grow up. Vilamil had heard Kaldarye say that often enough but until this moment had never actually beleived it. 
<br />

<br />
The lichen illuminating Neriak's ceiling glowed eerily. And a lone giant bat soared overhead. Vilamil tucked his irridescent white hair under his cloak and removed his lockpicks from his bag. A soft sensation of sorrow wafted through his chest. He didn't want the child to die any more than he wanted Kaldarye to. He wanted Keeah to live the life of a normal tier'dal. He was fond of her. He tried to shake the feeling off. Feelings were dangerous. A weakness he could ill afford when gods were involved. He moved down the street quickly trying to focus on the rescue. He'd deal with the child and her mother once they were safely back on Rallos Zek. 
<br />

<br />
The enchanter's house was dimly lit. There were fewer guards then there'd been when Kaldarye had been taken away, but in their place several sonic wolves now wandered about. Knowing that there were only two direct entries into the house, Vilamil selected the one less likely to be watched for intrusion. Taking care not to be seen, Vilamil approached the house from the back. The guards here did not appear to be alert. Dropping pepper powder on the ground to confuse the wolves sense of smell, Vilamil stealthily approached the house and detected several traps. He frowned. It would take a while to remove them all if he wanted to slip inside undetected. However to get Keeah out alive there was no choice and he gingerly took down the wards on the first trap. 
<br />

<br />
As he worked he couldn't shake the feeling that something ominous inside was about to happen.
<br />
