<br />
 
<br />
Lissa d'Malsqueek put a comforting hand on Keeah's brow and gently stroked her face as the child woke up. Her green eyes narrowed. Her concern for Keeah was anything but honorable. Soon this charade of masquerading as a high elf would be finished and she could return to her neglected social life. She cast a quick glance at her uncle Malsqueek, the enchanter. Inny's balls how she hated him for making her a high elf and taking on the tapestry of repression and slavery. It was almost as bad as the time he'd beat her for losing her virginity to a common street guard. At least the guard had given her some pleasure in return. But taking part in this high elven charade and illusion was horrible. Yet, what choice did she have. Without her uncle and his house and name, she was nothing. She needed his connection and name to marry herself out of his control and this hate forsaken house. It was bad enough pretending to be a high elf, but now that Keeah was under their roof, her uncle insisted she take on the roll 24 hours a day lest the child discover it was all a ruse. It galled Lissa to act the part of a slave. She was used to giving orders, not taking them. 
<br />

<br />
It was all her uncle Malsqueek's fault they were in this situation. He'd spent off the family fortune on frivolous things to impress the other houses. Gone were the trappings of wealth that once adorned the walls and floors. It had all been sold off to finance his lifestyle. And now that the family was bordering on being destitute her uncle decided it was time she and her brother Toosit earn their keep by going out and adventuring and acquiring great magical items to sell. Unfortunately neither she nor her brother possessed much of an ability to succeed in this endeavor and so her uncle had gone looking for someone who could and be easily controlled. That is when they'd discovered Keeah. Keeah was a natural spellcaster. She had more ability than both Lissa and Toosit put together. And with a few well placed minor poisons they'd managed to keep Keeah's unsuspecting and uncaring mother out of the picture, leaving Keeah with most of her afternoons free to be taught. 
<br />

<br />
Lissa smiled. Keeah's training was coming along quite nicely. She was a natural and innocent learner. But with her mother planning on leaving Legends and returning to Rallos Zek, it was now time to act to make sure the child lost her identity. 
<br />

<br />
She grinnned. Once Keeah accepted the slave collar of her own free will, she'd be bound to Malsqueek forever. 
<br />

<br />
The enchanter moved closer and shook Keeah. “This is taking too long. Wake her up. I want her loyalty to this house sealed.” 
<br />

<br />
Lissa kept her voice even. “The child has had quite a shock. Elven children are of delicate constitution. The stun spell her mother threw could have killed her.” 
<br />

<br />
“I don't care what it could have done. She's still alive and I'm getting tired. Get this business over with. I have important things to attend.” Malsqueek demanded. 
<br />

<br />
Lissa ground her teeth and held her tongue. Her uncle was on the verge of ruining everything. “Alright. But you'll have to make yourself invisible so the child doesn't see you.” She moved so that she was blocking the child's view of the dangerous enchanter and softly started speaking. “Come little one, wake up you've had a shock.” 
<br />

<br />
“Mama...” Keeah slurred as she struggled to wake up. 
<br />

<br />
“I'm here, it's me, Lissa.” 
<br />

<br />
Keeah blinked and looked around at the shabby walls and floor. “My mama, she was here. I saw her.” 
<br />

<br />
“No child it was just a bad dream. You fell and hit you're head.” 
<br />

<br />
Keeah rubbed her eyes and looked at Lissa as if she didn't know whether or not to believe her. She tried to sit up but Lissa pushed her back down. “Lay down little one. You need your strength. You have a long journey ahead.” 
<br />

<br />
“Where am I going?” 
<br />

<br />
“Back to Rallos Zek, remember?” 
<br />

<br />
Keeah looked alarmed. “But you said I didnt have to go. You said I could stay here if I got my mama to put on a collar.” 
<br />

<br />
Lissa smiled. Her plan was working. “That's right I did say that right before you fell. Do you really still want to stay with us.” 
<br />

<br />
“Yes I do, you're my only friends I have.” 
<br />

<br />
“Then you must put on this collar.” Lissa said as she handed the delicate silver linked chain to the child. 
<br />

<br />
Keeah's hands fumbled with the restraining device. She looked at it curiously then awkwardly put it on. As soon as the clasp clicked, Malsqueek appeared and said, “It's done. She's ours.” 
<br />

<br />
“Yes. She is.” Lissa replied as she let her own high elven illusion drop. 
<br />

<br />
“What?” Keeah said as she looked back and forth at the two tier'dal, Lissa and Malsqueek “Lissa, you're not a high elf anymore. You tricked me.” 
<br />

<br />
Malsqueek slapped the little girl so hard she fell to the floor. “So we did. Lissa was fooling you. And you fell for it. And now that the collar was put on your neck by your own hands and free will you belong to me. Welcome to your new family.” 
<br />

<br />
Keeah tried to protest but found her voice didn't work. It wasn't saying what she wanted. She wanted to cry for her mother but instead she felt the collar humming and her own voice silenced. All she could do was cry softly and hope he didn't hit her again.
<br />
