<br />

<br />
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
<br />
 
<br />
The prison was cold and curiously silent, like the hush of a faceless crowd awaiting the fall of an executioners gleaming blade onto the neck of an evil king's mistress. The scent of urine and blood clung to the air. 
<br />

<br />
Laying face down on the straw caked floor, Kaldarye Ka'Duceus did not move when she regained consciousness. She waited in hope that this was all a bad dream. Her head felt as if it had been smashed by a battering ram. She blinked trying to breath. Bright dewdrops on cobwebs seemed to flutter within one eye; the other was swollen shut. She sucked deep breaths of the stagnant air tasting wet straw and blood; she grimaced at the caustic tang of it. 
<br />

<br />
Gradually her vision cleared. With her good eye she looked around. Blood splattered smokey stone walls loomed next to her like a the sides of a sarcophagus. She felt as if she were trapped in her own grave. She didn't know where she was. Frantically she said a prayer to Innoruuk, calling for the familiar prescense of the dark god and not relaxing til she felt his unearthly hate at her side. She tried to sit up but pain sliced down her right side from shoulder to chest. The shock of it made her forget everything but the pain and she screamed. Her scream did not sound like her own. It was dry, raspy, high pitched like a rat under a hungry cat's paws. 
<br />

<br />
Kaldaye licked her swollen pussy lips and said a prayer to Innoruuk to heal her broken body. She didn't have a lot of mana. Not nearly enough to heal herself completely and to escape. She could do one or the other, heal or escape. A solid nights rest would require full mana return and for that she didn't have time. She took stock of her situation. Her epic dangled off one broken arm but the bones were mendable with a few well placed spells. However with her less than perfect healing abilities it would take a few weeks in a sling to heal properly. She still had her spells memorized so she could at least get out of this forbidding place. She blinked as a thunderous realization came over her. 
<br />

<br />
Malsqueek the enchanter was an idiot. She tried to smile through cracked lips as the memories of her combat at his house rushed back at her. The stupid enchanter had failed to take precautions to make sure she lacked her spells before he'd had her taken away to this dungeon. All she had to do to get out of this cramped cell was gate. 
<br />

<br />
Without another thought she cast the spell and landed back at the inn she, Keeah and Vilamil had made their home on Legends. Seeing a light on in the kitchen, Kaldarye limped in on Vilamil sporting a new guildtag, nursing a bottle of wine looking highly annoyed. 
<br />

<br />
He gave her a curt once-over when she entered the room and took a seat next to him. The rogue appeared disheveled and annoyed as if he'd been slinking around in some bushes and didn't find what he was seeking. A few sticks and leaves clung to his black tunic. The gray grease he used to slick back his hair gave him a sinister speculative air. And the way he was looking at her with a hooded expression reminded her of the first time she'd gone to visit Earenhylle. "I just sent a message to Rallos Zek to your husband.” He said giving her a sideways glance. 
<br />

<br />
Kaldarye emotions nearly exploded.“Why, would you do that?” She tried not to appear interested in his answer as she carefully layed her broken arm on the table. The bones were crooked and one in her wrist was poking out of her skin. It was going to be painful to straighten. 
<br />

<br />
“To inform him of our return to Rallos.” 
<br />

<br />
Kaldarye's head snapped up. She jerked her broken arm sending a spear of pain up her neck. Her voice squeaked with pain. “You did what?” 
<br />

<br />
Vilamil looked amused. He took a swallow of his wine. “Greon knows of our return.” he said smugly. 
<br />

<br />
Kaldarye felt as if a swirling hole to hell had opened up on the tiled floor beneath her. Her stomach twisted. “How could you do that... to me? All these years, he probably thought I was dead.” 
<br />

<br />
“He doesn't think that anymore.” 
<br />

<br />
Kaldarye was torn between wanting to tear the rogue's eyes out and asking him why he'd informed Greon she was still alive. The light in his blue eyes was too amused for her to ask and Kaldarye reminded herself that Vilamil always operated on his own agenda. She studied him trying to appear nonchalant, but burning with the desire to ask him why he'd contacted Greon. Yet she didn't want too appear to interested and ask him, although had she been able to look at herself she would have known that her annoying glare and her sudden interest in her arm gave her curiosity away. 
<br />

<br />
Vilamil chuckled. "You'll get to see him soon." 
<br />

<br />
His assuming demeanor made Kaldarye even more determined to ignore him. She straightened her arm on the table. Setting it would be painful. “I hate Greon. Don't talk about him, I need to concentrate.”. 
<br />

<br />
Vilamil leaned towards her. “You're not going to set that yourself are you?” 
<br />

<br />
“Of course I'm going to set it myself. Why wouldn't I?” 
<br />

<br />
He looked at her curiously. “It looks like a bad break. You've told me yourself, your forte as a cleric is not self-healing.” 
<br />

<br />
“Your concern is noted.” Kaldarye snapped. She wished she'd thought to grab a bottle of whiskey before she'd come upon the rogue; not to give to him or herself, but to cuckold him over the head with. 
<br />

<br />
She eyed the bones, and decided she'd do a quick set and just get them all inside her skin, and pay Greon's temple a fortune to figure out how to set them all properly when she returned to Rallos Zek. 
<br />

<br />
She closed her eye, wishing, she'd had the mana to take down the swelling on the closed one, and layed her good hand on her arm. The sudden twist and snap as she forced bone inside her skin hurt more than she expected and kaldarye had to grind her teeth to keep from crying out. She held her arm close to her as hundreds of memories of all the times, she'd been tortured as a child came flying back at her. 
<br />

<br />
Vilamil watched her saying nothing. And when she was finished and reeling from the pain, he slipped around behind her and put his hands on the back of her neck. “It's time to go to sleep, priestess, while I go fetch your daughter.” 
<br />

<br />
Kaldarye wanted to ask him what he was talking about, what about Keeah. For a moment her child's sweet face with the sparkling blue eyes and silky white curls flashed across her mind, but it vanished as hundreds of shadows gathered in front of her eyes plunging her into darkness. 
<br />

<br />
Vilamil caught Kaldarye before she hit the floor. He carried her over to her bed and tied her there. She was not going to escape him and get into any mischief again, not at least til they had both returned to Rallos. He looked down and studied her. Knocking her out while she was in pain had been easy. And with Kaldarye asleep it would be simple to transport her over the server-zone. He pondered the coming dawn and the duel. Was there any reason now for him to go back and rescue Keeah?
<br />
