<br />
 
<br />
In the windowless bedchamber a dozen sconces held the shadows at bay. The only noise was a flicking sound and a low murmur in tier'dal from outside the room. 
<br />

<br />
Kaldarye lay perfectly still even though she was uncomfortable. The straw bed on which she lay was too hard on sore and swollen body. She wished the bed were deep seated, feathered and soft, with downy covers and a lacy canopied curtain to keep out wandering eyes; something befitting a high priestess of Innoruuk and not an acolyte or apprentice of the blood faith. She thought of the bed that Tramplebrey had; king sized, claw footed, with a deep blue silk draping the sides. There was enough room in that bed for ten barbarian harem slaves. She'd like a bed like that except in red, the color of blood and daintily embroidered with a gigantic likeness of Innoruuk's balls. 
<br />

<br />
The voices outside drew closer. Kaldarye slitted her eyes and glanced towards the sound - a halfing nurse sat at her bedside busily clicking knitting needles through a trail of brown yarn in short rapid stacatto. The halfing's eyes were closed and she gently swayed as she worked. Behind the halfing, in the doorway of the room were two tier'dal, one was Vilamil and the other was Greon. 
<br />

<br />
Kaldarye's surprise was too genuine to hide. Innoruuk's tongue how had she gotten here, home to Rallos Zek. She struggled to remember all that had happened, but her last thoughts had been on her broken arm and Keeah. 
<br />

<br />
“Keeah,” she said remembering her daughter. Her voice was raspy like an iksar's hiss. 
<br />

<br />
The halfing immediately set down her knitting, nodded to the two Tier'dal in the opening and and put a mug holding a sweet smelling tea to Kaldarye's lips. “Drink this up m'lady, it will heal your ills.” 
<br />

<br />
The tea smelled as if it had been laced with persimmons, apricots and that foul smelling cognac stuff that Greon fancied. Kaldarye tried to push the concoction away, but her arms were heavy and wrapped in splints. She gagged as the tangy drink rushed down her throat. After swallowing it, she felt warm and sleepy. 
<br />

<br />
“Times are difficult. Neriak is at war with the light races.” one of them said. Greon or Vilamil, she coudn't tell, the drug was distorting her senses. 
<br />

<br />
“You must keep her out of it. No one knows she is back.” 
<br />

<br />
“Will be difficult. The war involves Innoruuk and both temples.” 
<br />

<br />
“Do whatever you must. But keep her out of my way.” 
<br />

<br />
“What is your interest in my wife.” that voice had to be Greon, but the question was never answered as she felt herself pulled past consciousness and into that black place beyond hate where Innoruuk lay. 
<br />

<br />
Kaldarye found herself drug into a room, without walls or floors, a shapless mass of starlined shadows surrounding a black four poster bed. The bed was similar to Tramplebrey's bed but gigantic sized, with a mattress stuffed covered with a blood red coverlet embroidered with thousands of squirming high elves. 
<br />

<br />
On top of the bed was Innoruuk, naked, grotesque and throbbing. Next to the bed was a chessboard with dark and light race pieces on it. The pieces on the chessboard she recognized as herself, Earenhylle, Greon, Tramplebrey, Viertania, Micheall, and some of the other light races from Greon's guild. There were more pieces on the board than the game normally allowed and each one looked lifelike. Innoruuk picked up the piece that represented her before her face was burned with acid and licked it with his long green tongue. He then set it back down next to Tramplebrey's enrobed piece. 
<br />

<br />
Cold hatefilled eyes narrowed filling her with both dread and awe. “Come demonstrate your loyalty and hate, my priestess.” his voice thundered.
<br />
