<br />
The Duel 
<br />

<br />
The morning was still dark. It was only an hour till sunrise but the clear patch of sky near the wizard teleport in Nektulos forest gleamed brightly with an orange crescent moon and a thousand twinkling stars. Near the teleport the grassy hillside was slick with dew. The enchanter will show at any moment, Vilamil thought as he readied his mind for battle. Battle on open ground was not where Vilamil would have chosen to fight an enchanter but since Vilamil had issued the challenge, his opponent decided the terms. 
<br />

<br />
The fight was to the death. 
<br />

<br />
Vilamil scoffed. He didn't think Kaldarye's whelp was worth the effort but if the child was left behind, he knew Kaldarye would someday try and return to Legends and that could have disastrous consequences. If she returned his own plans with Kaldarye would be at stake. He frowned. The priestess was too fanatically single-minded - once she got in her head to do something. Someone needed to rein her in. 
<br />

<br />
Peels of drunken laughter caught his interest and he glanced towards the sound. A few young revelers, tier'dal adventurers he'd met before the duel in the Blind Fish, walked into the clearing. The previous night the quartet returned from the Paludal Caves of Luclin and were sharing their wine and their battle stories to anyone who would listen. Vilamil feigned interest and causally mentioned the impending duel with Malsqueek to see if he'd get a reaction. “He's an elf lord enchanter. A noble. I've seen him down at the guild.” said the youngest of the quartet. Vilamil then told them he was going to duel Malsqueek and the youngster had added, “He's mean. He brags of all the people he's slain, and he's very wealthy. He has a staff made out of gold.” Vilamil lifted a brow. He had seen the inside of Malsqueeks home and knew that its interior was dusty and devoid of anything of value. Strange that this youngster had the opposite impression. Now in the morning twilight the adventuring Tier'dal crossed the hillside, and the youngest saluted him with a skin of wine while his 3 companions spread their cloaks on the ground. 
<br />

<br />
Vilamil saluted back with his dagger, then studied the terrain surrounding the spires. Other than the hillside, the ground was flat and grassy with no place to corner an opponent. The fight was going to be a lot of running till he could get the caster close enough to engage, but as long as the enchanter was running he wouldn't be casting spells, and Vilamil would have the advantage of facing the enchanter's back. He'd already prepared a poison that would lightly sting his skin that he would quafe right before the start of the duel to ward off Malsqueek's stuns and mezzes. His eyes glanced towards the wizard spires and to the dark trees of Nekutulos behind it. The battle would be his once he lured the enchanter into striking range. 
<br />

<br />
All for a child. Kaldarye's child. His expression blackened at the memory of Keeah's silky haired blue-eyed face. Before he returned her mother he intended to do a little investigating into her parentage. A small faction of the Hated Ones were still dwelling in the city on Legends, and with the right persuasion he would get their help. Depending on who Keeah's father was, she may yet have her uses. 
<br />

<br />
The moon and stars waned as the sun struggled to make an appearance. Vilamil wanted this combat finished before the first light of day and he glanced into Nektulos impatiently. 
<br />

<br />
Coming through the trees, he saw 2 figures approach, Malsqueek and his nephew Toosits. Malsqueek was dressed in flowing cream robes, embroidered on the cuffs and down the center with black and gold circles. A satin cape dyed shade of Iceclad ocean enveloped his shoulders. The cape was inlaid with topaz gemstones that twinkled when he walked. Matching silver bracers in the shape of coiled snakes encirled each wrist and on each hand he wore 5 large golden rings. His bluish gray hair was free flowing and surrounding his forehead he wore a thin golden circlet studded with rubies. Vilamil appraised the enchanters attire for value, and in his opinion the enchanter was overdressed. His eyes shifted to Toosits, the enchanter's nephew and one of the elves that had befriended and betrayed Keeah. The younger tier'dal wore a thin gray cotton robe with no ornamentation or jewelry. His eyes darted around and he shivered but from cold or excitement, Vilamil couldn't tell. 
<br />

<br />
The enchanter stopped in front of the wizard spires. “He who dares challenge me, reveal yourself, or hide like the coward you are.” 
<br />

<br />
Vilamil stepped from the shadows. Mentally he checked his inventory of equipment. His resists were high, over 300 without buffs and he knew he would need it for the fight ahead. The information he'd obtained at the Blind Fish gave him the impression the enchanter was skilled, which meant that Vilamil had to hope the enchanter fell for the carefully laid ruse, that he was in a low level guild and not in one that was plane of time flagged. With any luck Malsqueek would take his new guildtag at face value and had not put any research into his activities on Legends. 
<br />

<br />
Malsqueek stared down his long pointed nose at the rogue. “You come with no second?” 
<br />

<br />
Gesturing to the hillside, Vilamil pointed at a small crowd of onlookers. 
<br />

<br />
Malsqueek frowned. “This duel was to be done in private.” 
<br />

<br />
“There is no limit on the number of seconds.” 
<br />

<br />
“I am imposing a limit.” 
<br />

<br />
“The time to impose limits is past. 
<br />

<br />
“You are violating terms.” 
<br />

<br />
“There were no terms other than the time and location.” 
<br />

<br />
“You are trying to alter the agreement. You waste my time.” Malsqueek said. 
<br />

<br />
“Then you forfeit?” 
<br />

<br />
“Don't be ridiculous. You are the one violating the duel. The forfeit is yours.” 
<br />

<br />
Vilamil drew a dagger with a long curved shank. He casually ran his fingers over the blade..“I am here and willing to fight at your place of choosing at your appointed hour.” 
<br />

<br />
“I will not be threatened or spoken to so disrespectfully by a mere commoner. I am a noble of Neriak.”the enchanter said with a sweep of his cape. He turned his back and started walking back towards the trees. 
<br />

<br />
“Fight me or forfeit.” Vilamil replied eyeing the enchanter's back speculatively. “The first strike is yours.” 
<br />

<br />
The enchanter kept walking. 
<br />

<br />
“Cross the line of trees and my witnesses will report your cowardice and forfeit, while I'll go collect the child.” 
<br />

<br />
Malsqeek stopped and turned around. He glared at Vilamil with murderous intent. “You wouldn't dare. I am a noble of Neriak.” 
<br />

<br />
“I am a rogue of Rallos Zek.” 
<br />

<br />
“Have you no honor?” 
<br />

<br />
“Fight me or forfeit.” Vilamil replied. 
<br />

<br />
“So be it. But when you die I'll do everything I can to it permanent.” He nodded to his nephew then reached for the clasp on his cape as if to take it off; but instead of taking off the cape he slipped one hand up his sleeve and withdrew a wand. 
<br />

<br />
Used to slight of hand tricks Vilamil stood his ground and allowed the enchanter to strike him. The wand lit the sky with a blue bolt of energy. The bolt hit Vilamil in the chest and knocked him backwards. Vilamil's skin felt as if it had been saturated with hot peppers that rapidly dissipated till he was left with an over all tingling. He rolled to the ground pretending he was injured by the blast to gage the enchanter's response. His own lack of damage from the wand and the fact that the enchanter used it rather than his own spells gave Vilamil a strong suspicion that the enchanter wasn't as good at combat as was rumored. 
<br />

<br />
Vilamil stopped rolling and lay still. He could hear the squish of the enchanters soft leather boots on the grass. He slivered one eye open and watched. Malsqueek approached his body. There was a mixture of shock and triumph in his eyes. He poked Vilamil's body with his staff. Vilamil held still. 
<br />

<br />
“You are beneath my talent, Dog.” Malsqeek said. “You should never have given me first strike. Now the duel is over and you lay dead. I claim victory.” He flung his cap over his shoulders with disdain and turned towards the adventurers on the hill, giving Vilamil a clean shot at his back.. 
<br />

<br />
“Here is your champion.” Malsqueek gloated. “Leave now and spread word of my victory or I shall fell your heads next.” 
<br />

<br />
Behind the enchanter, Vilamil silently got to his feet and drew two daggers. He stabbed both of them up into the enchanter's back. Malsqueek arched his spine. He screamed. Not withdrawing the blades, Vilamil twisted them upwards under the enchanter's ribs and into his beating heart. The enchanter gurgled as blood spewed from his mouth. Vilamil twisted the daggers again. He waited till Malsqueek stopped moving then lowered his arms and let the body slide off his blades. The body hit the ground with a soft “wumpf.” 
<br />

<br />
The enchanter was dead. 
<br />

<br />
Vilamil turned towards Toosit and the young mage started running. Giving chase he followed him all the way back to Neriak. Near the city gate he caught him and put a dagger to his back. “Lead me to Keeah.” He said into Toosit's ear. Terrified the mageling complied. 
<br />

<br />
Malsqueek's house was as dark and barren as he remembered. The enchanter was inside at his bind. He was wearing a simple lilac robe and was holding a ornately carved brass box. 
<br />

<br />
Now he wasn't so arrogant but Vilamil still didn't trust him. 
<br />

<br />
Looking at the box suspiciously Vilamil said. “Where is the child.” 
<br />

<br />
“She isn't here.” 
<br />

<br />
Vilamil felt Toosit stiffen. The mageling tried to pull away but Vilamil kept a firm grip on him. “Where is she?” 
<br />

<br />
Malsqueeks eyes raked derisively over Toosit. “Let my nephew go, he is not worth the blood he'd spill.” 
<br />

<br />
Ignoring him, Vilamil said. “Where is Keeah?” 
<br />

<br />
“She is in a safe place. Perhaps you'd consider a trade? One mageling for another? I have a niece and a nephew. You could have your pick.” 
<br />

<br />
“You nephew or your niece don't interest me.” 
<br />

<br />
“Then perhaps I can offer you something of value in exchange.” Malsqueek said with a gentle pat on the box. 
<br />

<br />
Vilamil slanted his glance. “There isn't anything you have that would equal Keeah.” 
<br />

<br />
Malsqueek snorted. “She is only a child. A child can only be put to so much use.” a thought occurred to the enchanter. He gave Vilamil a pointed stare. “Unless you are claiming she is noble.” 
<br />

<br />
“Keeah is of priestly descent.” 
<br />

<br />
“Then she is worthless. You'll get no reward. I tested her myself. Her appitude is arcane. Her family won't want her.” 
<br />

<br />
“Enough of your rambling. Bring her to me.” Vilamil said as he started to slice into Toosit's ear. 
<br />

<br />
The mageling screamed and tried to twist away. Vilamil slammed him into the wall and pinned his arm behind his back. He kneed the mageling in the spine to stun him, then kicked his legs out from under him. 
<br />

<br />
“Uncle.” Toosit called as Vilamil forced him to his knees and stood over him. The mageling was cowering and wimpering in pain. 
<br />

<br />
Malsqueek watched Vilamil easily overpower his nephew with alarming speed. He licked his lip nervously. He had no intention of being next. “Stop at once and you can have this for the child.” the enchanter said opening the box. 
<br />

<br />
Suspecting a trick, Vilamil gave the box a sideways glance. Nestled on a pool of maroon velvet lay an egg sized opague stone filled with swirling white smoke. Within the smoke burned a small flame. Vilamil nearly dropped his knife at the sight of it. He stared openmouthed. Never in his wildest imaginings had he envisioned Malsqueek offering him a phylactory of a soul essence. If that was what this item was then it was priceless. He'd only seen one other like it, and it was in the possession of his liege D'Jaren N'bane. 
<br />

<br />
His gray eyes lifted to the face of the enchanter. 
<br />

<br />
Malsqueeks black eyes fixed on Vilamil's with unnerving intensity. “Release my nephew.” 
<br />

<br />
Vilamil had no idea how Malsqueek had come across such an item. Magic such as this was highly guarded and prized by the upper houses of Neriak. Legend had it that the phylactory's couldn't be reproduced because Innoruuk himself had trapped the soul inside. Moreover the stones could only be used by elves of certain noble lineage, family lines that Innoruuk had marked. If anyone else tried the essence inside would kill them. In the old days it was the way upper families weeded false heirs. But with the decline of Tier'dal births the practice had stopped. He raked his gaze across the stone. It had to be a fake. There was no way Malsqeek could be in possession of it. 
<br />

<br />
Vilamil looked at Malsqueek doubtfully. “Summon its essence and prove to me it's not a fake.” 
<br />

<br />
Malsqueek hesitated. “I can't.” 
<br />

<br />
“I see.” Vilamil replied as he made another slice in Toosit's ear. 
<br />

<br />
“Wait you don't understand. Here the item is worthless, the insideous nature of its' magic fails to function properly,” the enchanter continued quickly almost to the point of babbling, “but on Rallos Zek, where it originated I believe you'd find it useful.” 
<br />

<br />
Vilamil narrowed his eyes. “It should work as well here as on rallos.” 
<br />

<br />
Malsqueek licked his lips, “For a native of Rallos. But I am not. You however are...that is if you've the will to use it.” The enchanter's eyes gleamed and a bit of a sneer cracked into his voice. 
<br />

<br />
Not trusting him, Vilamil replied, “Enough of your lies. It is a fake.” 
<br />

<br />
“No it is not. Hold it. Touch it, call forth the essence of hatred. Hold Innoruuk's power in your hand.” 
<br />

<br />
Vilamil was tempted but suspecting a trap he held his fingers back. 
<br />

<br />
“I suppose that a rogue such as yourself would lack the will.” He pulled the box away and his eyes snapped to his nephew. “Toosits, go get your sister to fetch the child.” 
<br />

<br />
Not letting Toosits go, Vilamil returned the enchanter's stare. “We had an agreement. Now you bring me Keeah or I remove the rest of this mageling's ears.” 
<br />

<br />
Malsqueek offered the open box again. “Change the agreement. Take the stone.” 
<br />

<br />
Vilamil stared at the swirling smoke surrounding the flame. He couldn't tell whether or not the item was real. Had D'Jaren N'bane offered it to him he wouldn't have questioned it. Smoke and flame inside a stone was difficult if not impossible to recreate, and he'd already seen enough of Malsqeek's skill on the battle field to know that Malsqueek didn't have the talent to manufacture this kind of illusion. He hesitated. The odds were in his favor that the item was a fake, but if he was wrong and the stone housed a real Tier'dal soul. Then the item was priceless. He looked down at Toosit, thought briefly of Keeah and all the times she'd wandered off and disobeyed, then back at the stone. The temptation was unbearable. Kaldarye could have other children. Releasing the mageling, he reached into the box and picked it up. 
<br />

<br />
Malsqueek was laughing. “Now you die.” he said as he backed into the shadowy hallway. 
<br />

<br />
The stone vibrated in Vilamil's hand; warm, pulsing, like a living heart. For a moment nothing happened then smoke curled out of it and the image of a tier'dal female appeared. She was wearing chain armor, and wielding two curving swords. At her waist several different colored wands lined her belt. Her silvery hair flowed down past her hips to her knees the way the females of the old lore wore it. In truth she resembled a high elf with inky blue skin. She was tall, her features sharply angled, her golden eyes very catlike. She looked at Vilamil pointedly. 
<br />

<br />
Below him, Toosits scooted away, making his escape. 
<br />

<br />
Ignoring him, Vilamil watched the essence closely. 
<br />

<br />
She turned towards him and lifted her golden swords. “I am N'hahraj d'Sharhendra. You who summoned me. Prepare for Innoruuk's teeth in your flesh.” 
<br />

<br />
“Kill him the way you slew the previous lord of this house.” Malsqeek's voice whispered through shadows. 
<br />

<br />
There was not a chance of Vilamil permitting that. Addressing the essence the way he'd watched D'Jaren, Vilamil said. “Your will is mine. Your blood is mine. Stand down and obey my word.” 
<br />

<br />
The essence hovered over him for a few seconds then she lowered her swords and her head. Vilamil commanded her back into the stone and she disappeared. 
<br />

<br />
“No.” Malsqueek said. The enchanter crept closer. He stared at vilamil in disbelief. “She was supposed to slay you. Who are you? You tricked me.” 
<br />

<br />
Slipping the stone into his pack, Vilamil turned to the enchanter and replied, “I accept the stone as trade for Keeah.”
<br />
