<br />
The Hovel 
<br />

<br />
Kaldarye was trapped. She wasn't tied down but she may as well have been. Both her arms and one of her legs was splinted making it damn impossible to move. This had to be a cruel ironic joke of Greon and the halfing nurse guard. She wasnt even sure how long she'd lain here in the bed but she knew it had to be days, perhaps even weeks. What was worse was that she was unable to gather any mana. It was if some unseen alien force was constantly draining her. Kaldarye thought it was the halfing's tangy tea, even the idea of drinking any more made her want to vomit. 
<br />

<br />
She turned her head towards the halfing nurse. “My arm hurts.” 
<br />

<br />
“I expect it does.” the halfing replied as she continued knitting and making the incessant clicking. The sound was getting on Kaldarye's nerves. It was like a woodpecker that wouldn't quit. 
<br />

<br />
“I need a message sent.” 
<br />

<br />
“What you need is rest.” 
<br />

<br />
“Who are you to tell me anything?” 
<br />

<br />
“I'm Nilla Hoppenpopper, your nurse and if you don't mind me saying, you took quite a tumble.” the halfing replied without missing a beat in her knitting. 
<br />

<br />
Kaldarye tried to sit up but the bindings on her arms and leg made it impossible. She felt so encumbered. She needed someone to cut her free. 
<br />

<br />
“Where's Keeah?” Her daughter would make a better messenger than this halfing. 
<br />

<br />
“Keeah, my lady?” 
<br />

<br />
“My child.” 
<br />

<br />
“Your child isn't yet born.” the hafling tisked as she put a porcelain teacup to her lips. “You're getting confused. Now drink some tea.” 
<br />

<br />
“No,” Kaldarye said, spitting the acrid brew back at the halfing. “Keep that foul potion away.” 
<br />

<br />
“But M'lady it will heal you.” the halfing said as she began patting up the mess with the ragged edge of her skirt. 
<br />

<br />
“I don't want it.” 
<br />

<br />
“Perhaps your wanting something else then? I bit of warm milk with brandy? Some cocatrice soup? I think I can arrange that with the cook if you agree to take a little tea.” 
<br />

<br />
“I want a cleric to heal me.” 
<br />

<br />
“You'll have to settle for tea,” the hafling said sticking the mug back in Kaldarye's face. She tilted Kaldarye's head back and tried to force her to drink. “There's not any clerics around.” 
<br />

<br />
I”m a cleric. 
<br />

<br />
“Ahhhh. Then heal up yourself.” the halfing said as she sat back on her chair and watched expectantly. 
<br />

<br />
Kaldarye hesitated. “I can't.” 
<br />

<br />
“Hmmmmm?” 
<br />

<br />
“I don't have mana.” 
<br />

<br />
“Well then you'll just have to meditate, and rest up.” 
<br />

<br />
“Med, that's all I've done is med. I”m tired of medding. I want my child and real healing.” 
<br />

<br />
“Their isn't a healer in the house and your child” she patted Kaldarye's round stomach, “Well that's not due for a few more months.” 
<br />

<br />
Kaldarye glared at the halfling, “What kind of nurse are you if you can't magically heal.” 
<br />

<br />
“The kind that knows about her herbs and remedies is what. My great Grandmother Hypha was a mighty druidess of the Misty Thicket, she fought your dark evil kind, ran you out of our forests, she did. Great Grandmum was a heroine to the kindly folk of Rivervale. She and she passed down her remedies and potions to my Grandmum Printha who learned a great deal about herbalism from an elven druid named Aerenus. Grandmum Printha used to fight the undead in the evil Kithicor forest, that was before she met her husband Gorgo who was a farmer near the druids lairs in Rivervale.” 
<br />

<br />
“Innoruuk's blessed balls, Get me a cleric.” Kaldarye interrupted. 
<br />

<br />
“Oh I dare not. The master said your injuries are too extensive and you should heal up naturally. 
<br />

<br />
“Extensive? It's just my right arm. What's up with all the splints all over my body.” 
<br />

<br />
“Master Greon said you were beaten like an errant child.” 
<br />

<br />
“Greon.” Kaldarye's eyes fumed. She should have known Greon was behind this encapsulation. “Bring me Greon then, At least he tries to be a cleric.” 
<br />

<br />
“Oh he's out today, gone to the temple, he has. I don't expect him till later in the evening.” 
<br />

<br />
“Then go down to the temple and get him.” 
<br />

<br />
“Oh I dare not leave, not with all the trouble about halfbreeds and such. Safer for me in here. Besides, I'm working on a shawl for your little one.” 
<br />

<br />
A shadow of confusion crossed where Kaldarye's brow would have been were it not for the acid burns. “For Keeah?” 
<br />

<br />
“Keeah? Is that what you plan on naming it? Such a lovely sound to it.” 
<br />

<br />
“No, Keah is....nevermind. Where's Vilamil the rogue.” 
<br />

<br />
“Rogue m'lady? I don't know who your talking about. Mayhap your tired and would like a little tea?” the hafling said as she tried to force it into Kaldarye's throat. 
<br />

<br />
“No tea. Keep that foul stuff away from me. Bring me my child.” 
<br />

<br />
“I thought yee wanted a rogue. Now your asking about a child?” 
<br />

<br />
The hafling tilted kaldarye's head back.“Your child is not yet born, not for several months yet. I think you are confused. Now drink some tea.” 
<br />

<br />
Kaldarye glared at the halfing. If I drink this, you will pass a message on to Greon?” 
<br />

<br />
“Of course, M'lady, I am after all your servant.” 
<br />

<br />
“Some servant. I'm going to have a talk with Greon about SLAVES who don't know their place and use blackmail.” 
<br />

<br />
“Oh m'lady, I would never resort to blackmail. You must be mistaking me for someone else. The tea will clear your head.” 
<br />

<br />
Kaldarye glared at her. “Just pass a message on to Greon.” 
<br />

<br />
“As you wish.” 
<br />

<br />
Kaldarye took a swallow of the sweet blend and grimaced as it coated her throat. The brew seemed to be getting stronger and thicker every time she drank it. “Find out from Greon what happened to Keeah.” 
<br />

<br />
Nilla looked at her doubtfully. “If you wish, M'lady. I do think your speaking daffy.” 
<br />

<br />
“Your opinion isn't wanted.” 
<br />

<br />
“Well I do think your speaking out of your mind. In my opinion dark elves are too opinionated. What with your always wanting to kill each other and the rest of the races. You should really try gardening. A few flowers will brighten up your mood and your house.” 
<br />

<br />
Had the tea not been so fast acting Kaldarye would have thought of some snappy comeback but as usual right after she drank the tea, Kaldarye felt inordinantly sleepy. “Just do it.” she mumbled. She was thinking of sending Keeah a message but sleep overcame.
<br />
