				<td colspan="2"><span class="postbody">A few days after securing Kaldarye in Greon's house, Vilamil was seated in one of the private rooms of High Lord D'Jaren N'Bane.   Designed to conceal danger and give a false sense of comfort; D'jaren's receiving room was furnished with considerable style.  Two wing-backed chairs flanked a  wooden table covered with a damask cloth.  A red and gold rug covered the floor and on the table sat a crystal decanter filled with a dark amber brandy.  A pair of glasses etched with the talon crest and decorated with saphire and silver diamond-dust sat on either side of the decanter.  The walls were painted the deep indigo of the twilight sky. Near the table a small stone fireplace decorated with colored tiles was softly lit by a young fire elemental.  The light from the elemental bathed the  room with a warm glow that  Vilamil knew was deceiving.    The flickering light failed to illuminate the far end of the room seemingly the home to several large potted lichen shrubs.  The plants were an illusion and it was from this corner that Vilamil watched.  
<br />

<br />
Shadows draped a willowy shape that stepped through the illusion  – a dark elf, straight-backed and tall with a languid grace all the more compelling.  He walked towards Vilamil.  The shadows fell from him.  He was dressed in  a cobalt tabbard embroidered with the claw of a hawk.  The tabbard overlaid a shimmery tunic threaded with metallic silver. His cloak was othmir of deepest midnight and underbraided with siren's hairs.   His carriage was effortless and aloof belaying his power and his absolute confidence to succeed.  He wore his ivory hair slicked back over his pointed ears; and draping well past his shoulders to a tapered tip.  As he approached there was a sense of force in each long stride.  His carriage gave off the aura of someone used to getting whatever he wished.  He stopped several feet away.  Vilamil rose and made a cursory bow.
<br />

<br />
Lord D'jaren N'bane's icy gaze flicked over Vilamil's legend acquired armor, and glowed with rare approval.  “Innoruuk  favors you.” His voice was soft, melodic and deceptive.  Many elves had lost their lives by foolishly falling at ease at the sound of that voice.   Not waiting for Vilamil to reply he continued, “ you will need the Hated One's favor for the mission you undertake.”
<br />

<br />
Vilamil returned D'jaren's gaze and waited for the name of the hapless soul he'd be assigned to assassinate.  It was always like this.   A meeting, a name, terms, an assassination, and payment.   But this time D'Jaren surprised him.  He walked over to the table and poured himself a glass of the brandy.  He lifted the liquid to his lips, swirled it, sniffed it and put the glass back on the table near Vilamil and said in a surprisingly interested voice.  “Have you ever worshipped at the blood temple?”
<br />

<br />
It took all of Vilamil's fortitude to keep his expression passive.  “I have.”
<br />

<br />
D'jaren studied his face as he lifted the glass to his lips and took a sip of the brandy.  “We have ties, this house and that temple.  Ties of a deep binding nature.”
<br />

<br />
“You refer to your daughter, Kaldarye.”
<br />

<br />
Disapproval shot through D'Jaren's silky blue eyes and Vilamil could have stabbed himself for bringing her name up.  Living on Legends had made him forget.   D'Jaren was power.  He controlled the threads of life and death for many elves and Vilamil would do well to remember his elevated position in the house was tenuous at best.
<br />

<br />
“That errant child does not deserve the honor of my blood.”  D'jaren said thowing the glass into the wall.  Amber liquid and glass shattered.  
<br />

<br />
Vilamil couldn't agree more but he wisely held his words.   Kaldarye was beyond anything that he understood.  Living with her on Legends had only solidified his opinion.  She was mad - far too fanatical to Innoruuk for her own good.  Her kind treatment of Keeah was proof that her sanity had left her.  She had somehow warped from a Tier'dal priestess of Innoruuk to something devoid of the traditions of their people; yet still retaining their god's favor.  That was the biggest puzzle of all.  How did Kaldarye stay in Innoruuk's graces.   By all rights she should be more out of favor with Innoruuk than her husband was with King Thex..   
<br />

<br />
“She has defied me with her marriage contract to House Ka'Duceus.”  D'jaren said as  he stared at the wall at the spidery pattern of streaming liquid. “Measures have to be done to put an end to her rebellion.”
<br />

<br />
“Measures, m'lord?”  Vilamil asked wondering exactly what the clan lord had in mind. Surely he wasn't going to suggest Kaldarye's murder.  
<br />

<br />
As if reading the assassin's mind, D'Jaren turned back towards him and said, “Not a hair on her head is to be harmed.  She is still N'Bane.”  
<br />

<br />
Vilamil relaxed and then wondered who the mark was.  If not Kaldarye, then Greon  perhaps?  He didn't like that idea.   If he was ordered to kill Greon he'd have a difficult time placing Kaldarye in another secure location and still keep her hidden.  
<br />

<br />
D'jaren pivoted away from the wall.  The smell of liquor permeated the air behind him.  He turned towards Vilamil. “Your orders regarding her,  are to observe and protect.”
<br />

<br />
Removing all expression from his features Vilamil said, “As I always have.  You have another assignment in mind?”
<br />

<br />
D'jaren narrowed his cerulean eyes.  “Yes.”
<br />

<br />
Vilamil waited as his clan lord tilted his head to one side and gazed down at him with a calculated air.  “But this time I need not your skills as a killer.”
<br />

<br />
Vilamil lifted one brow in question. “Which of my skills did you have in mind, m'lord”
<br />

<br />
“Your ability to get into forbidden places.”
<br />

<br />
“So you need a rogue?”
<br />

<br />
“Not just any rogue.”  D'jaren's gaze was pointed.  “I need someone I can trust.”
<br />

<br />
Vilamil wanted to probe into his clan leader's mind.  But he was cautious.  He sensed it would rile his liege.  There was a philosophy when dealing with nobles to act submissive and weak.  Feed them simpering responsives and insincere flatteries.  Vilamil didn't subscribe to this approach.  He preferred to be non-threatening and establish rapport.   It was a better way to obtain information. “You know you have my loyalty.”
<br />

<br />
 D'Jaren's gaze seared him. “I need you to break into the Blood temple.”
<br />

<br />
Vilamil was intrigued.  His gray eyes narrowed in thought.  “Passage past the main hall in the blood temple is forbidden to those not ordained in the faith.” He rubbed his fingers together. “Anyone caught beyond the main hall of worship who is not a priest can expect  swift execution.”
<br />

<br />
“Which is why I need someone of your skill.”
<br />

<br />
Vilamil liked that D'Jaren recognized his talents.  “Consider it done.  What item am I retrieving?”
<br />

<br />
“Not an item, a person.”
<br />

<br />
Vilamil gave D'Jaren a questioning look.  “You wish me to abduct a priest?”
<br />

<br />
“Yes, I need you to bring my daughter to Talon House.   She has cloistered herself and refuses to come out.  It is impossible to scry or teleport into the Blood temple with magic.  The walls are protected.  Otherwise I'd have her brought here  myself.”
<br />

<br />
Vilamil lifted a brow intrigued.   “Surely my interference isn't necessary, you can order her out of that temple.  Summon her back to the clan.”
<br />

<br />
Djaren's eyes narrowed.  He clenched his fist.  “She is no longer mine to command.”
<br />

<br />
Feigning ignorance, Vilamil said, “She will not heed your word?”
<br />

<br />
D'Jaren's  cold eyes centered on Vilamil.  He felt all the hate and scorn the clanlord had for the situation with Kaldarye coming off in hot angry waves.  For the first time since Vilamil had arrived in Talon house that day, he was tempted to look away.  “Thus far she has chosen to refuse to obey  You should go prepared to use force.”
<br />

<br />
“You are expecting resistance?”  Open combat was something Vilamil astutely tried to avoid.  
<br />

<br />
“Yes, from Kaldarye and the other priests if you are discovered.”
<br />

<br />
“I've never been discovered.” <span style="font-style: italic"> He had no intention of ever being discovered.</span>
<br />

<br />
“This time you will be.”
<br />

<br />
Vilamil did not like that answer.  What was in D'Jaren's mind? He wondered.  An awareness of where Kaldarye really was?   Maybe somehow D'Jaren had learned that Vilamil had spend the past several months living with Kaldarye and this summons to Talon House was all a ruse.  He was beginning to think he'd made a very serious mistake in taking Kaldarye under his wing.  Was this a trap?  He wondered why D'jaren was trying to send him into battle.   He was a rogue, not a fighter. He did not want to put himself in a situation where he would get caught. Vilamil weighed his response.  He knew to refuse his clan lord's request was folly but to agree could ruin his career.  An idea occurred to him.  There was another possiblity.  Was the clan lord getting desperate.   He tried a different tactic to draw D'Jaren out to see which of the possiblities was the case.  “Have you considered laying siege to the temple, to force her.”
<br />
 
<br />
“Yes, actually, several times, but Innoruuk and the rest of Neriak would frown.”
<br />

<br />
Vilamil was unable to conceal his surprise.  Since when did D'Jaren ever care about the rest of Neriak.   Vilamil knew there was something going on that he wasn't being told.  Something that was making D'Jaren resort to hiring a rogue to get to Kaldarye.   But he could tell by the set of D'Jaren's jaw and the guarded look in his liege's eye that the information wasn't forthcoming.   “I can get to your daughter without drawing attention and shrink her to get her outside of the temple.  What are your instructions once I've done so.”
<br />

<br />
“To bring my daughter to me for an evening.    I will give you 200pp and lend you whatever magic you need to accomplish this task.”
<br />

<br />
“As you wish.” Vilamil replied with a nod of his head.<div style="vertical-align: bottom"><br /><br /><br />_________________<br />Kaldarye Ka'Duceus
